 Format
 ------
 The adjacency list format consists of lines with node labels.  The
 first label in a line is the source node.  Further labels in the line
 are considered target nodes and are added to the graph along with an edge
 between the source node and target node.
 
 The graph with edges a-b, a-c, d-e can be represented as the following
 adjacency list (anything following the # in a line is a comment)::
 
      a b c # source target target
      d e
+
+Node names with spaces
+----------------------
+A node name (``str(node)``) that is empty or holds whitespace, the
+delimiter, ``#``, ``"`` or ``\\`` is written in double quotes, with
+``\\`` and ``"`` escaped by a backslash and a line feed or carriage
+return written as ``\\n`` or ``\\r`` (``"New York"``, ``"say \\"hi\\""``).
+Readers take a token that starts with ``"`` as one quoted name, so such
+graphs round-trip. Edge data is written and read as before.
+
+Graph header
+------------
+With ``header=True`` the writers put a first line that names the type of
+the graph::
+
+ # networkx-graph: directed=True multigraph=False
+
+A graph with a name gets ``name="<name>"`` (a JSON string) at the end of
+the line. When ``create_using`` is None, the readers build a graph of that
+type (``Graph``, ``DiGraph``, ``MultiGraph`` or ``MultiDiGraph``), with that
+name, if such a line comes before the first data line. ``generate_*(G,
+header=True)`` yields the header line first, and
+:func:`~networkx.readwrite.edgelist.read_graph_header` reads only the
+header of a file.
+
+Errors
+------
+The ``parse_*`` and ``read_*`` functions raise
+:exc:`~networkx.LineParseError` (a ``TypeError``) for a line they cannot
+read. It says which line: ``line 3: Failed to convert ...``, and from a
+``read_*`` function with the file name, ``graph.txt:3: Failed to convert
+...``. Its attributes are `message`, `lineno` (from 1, comment and blank
+lines counted), `line` and `filename`.
+
+Sorted output
+-------------
+With ``sort=True`` the ``generate_*`` and ``write_*`` functions write the
+nodes, the neighbours of each node and parallel edges in the order of their
+``str``, an undirected edge from the end that sorts first. ``sort`` may also
+be a key function for the nodes, such as ``sort=lambda n: -G.degree(n)``;
+nodes with the same key keep the order of the graph. Two graphs with
+the same nodes and edges then give the same lines, whatever the order in
+which they were built.
+
+Compressed files
+----------------
+The ``read_*`` functions read a file compressed with gzip, bz2 or xz
+whatever its name: they recognise the compression by its first bytes. The
+``write_*`` functions take ``compression="gzip"``, ``"bz2"`` or ``"xz"`` to
+compress what they write, also into an open file; a file name ending in
+``.xz`` is compressed with xz, as ``.gz`` and ``.bz2`` are.
+
+Parsing
+-------
+The ``parse_*`` functions of the edge list, adjacency list and multiline
+adjacency list are :class:`~networkx.readwrite._lineparser.LineParser`
+methods: ``parse_edgelist(lines, comments, delimiter, nodetype, ...)`` is
+``LineParser(comments, delimiter, nodetype).parse_edgelist(lines, ...)``.
+
+Writing
+-------
+``generate_*`` of this module is a
+:class:`~networkx.readwrite._linewriter.LineWriter` method:
+``generate_adjlist(G, delimiter, ...)`` is
+``LineWriter(delimiter, ...).adjlist(G)``, and ``generate_pajek(G, ...)``
+is ``LineWriter(...).pajek(G)``.
+
+Files
+-----
+The ``read_*`` and ``write_*`` functions open, read and write their file
+through :class:`~networkx.readwrite._textfile.TextFile`:
+``TextFile(encoding).read(path, parse_*, ...)`` and
+``TextFile(encoding, ...).write(path, lines)``.
 """
 
 __all__ = ["generate_adjlist", "write_adjlist", "parse_adjlist", "read_adjlist"]
 
